      "claim": "The maximum-likelihood estimator (MLE) is the value of parameter $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $\\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^{n} p(x_i | \\theta)$ where $x_i$ are i.i.d. observations from a distribution with unknown parameter $\\theta$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:21:00\u201300:21:32] - The ASR states: 'So we have, like, the usual knowledge of the maximum likelihood estimator, as we've written in the chat. This is the first way that you have thought that you could learn on statistics courses. So we simply compute maximum likelihood estimation. So we're trying to maximize so-called likelihood function. And the likelihood function, in this case, is simply a product of those distributions where we substitute our observations in our density function.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The likelihood function is treated as a function of $\\theta$ rather than the observations $x_i$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:21:33\u201300:22:03] - The ASR states: 'So, pay attention that we treat this function not as a function of x's, because x's are null, but as a function of theta.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The log-likelihood function is defined as $\\ell(\\theta) = \\log \\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\log p(x_i | \\theta)$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:21:33\u201300:22:03] - The ASR states: 'Okay, so we simply take the product of those densities. Of course, in practice, the first thing we should do is to switch to a log likelihood in order to be able to maximize the sum of functions rather than [truncated]'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Taking the logarithm converts the product of probabilities into a sum, which is easier to maximize using calculus.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:21:33\u201300:22:03] - The ASR states: 'in order to be able to maximize the sum of functions rather than [truncated]' (implied that the product is harder to maximize than the sum)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The log-likelihood avoids numerical underflow issues.",
